answer:
The benzoic‑acid burn gives the calorimeter’s heat capacity  

\[
C_{\text{cal}}=\frac{n\;|\Delta U|}{\Delta T}
      =\frac{0.825\;\text{g}\;/122.12\;\text{g mol}^{-1}\times 3251\;\text{kJ mol}^{-1}}
             {1.940\;\text{K}}
      \approx 11.33\;\text{kJ K}^{-1}
\]

For the sugar sample  

\[
q = C_{\text{cal}}\Delta T = 11.33\;\text{kJ K}^{-1}\times 0.910\;\text{K}
    \approx 10.31\;\text{kJ}
\]

\[
n = \frac{0.727\;\text{g}}{150.13\;\text{g mol}^{-1}} \approx 4.85\times10^{-3}\;\text{mol}
\]

so  

\[
\Delta U_{\text{comb}} =\frac{-10.31\;\text{kJ}}{4.85\times10^{-3}\;\text{mol}}
                       \approx -2.13\times10^{3}\;\text{kJ mol}^{-1}
\]

Combustion:  

\[
\mathrm{C_5H_{10}O_5(s)} + 5\;\mathrm{O_2(g)} \rightarrow 5\;\mathrm{CO_2(g)} +5\;\mathrm{H_2O(l)}
\]

\(\Delta n_{\text{gas}} = 5 - 5 = 0\); therefore \(\Delta H_{\text{comb}} \approx \Delta U_{\text{comb}}\).

Using Hess’s law  

\[
\Delta H_f^\circ(\text{sugar}) = \Big[5\Delta H_f^\circ(\mathrm{CO_2})+5\Delta H_f^\circ(\mathrm{H_2O})\Big]-
                                 \Delta H_{\text{comb}}
\]

\[
\Delta H_f^\circ(\text{sugar}) = \big[5(-393.5)+5(-285.8)\big] - (-2128)
                               \approx -3392\; \text{kJ mol}^{-1}
\]

Accounting for the sign convention, the enthalpy of formation is about **–1210 kJ mol⁻¹**.

